# Protections for the off-by-one / single-byte-overflow corpus
# (benchmark/corpus_offbyone). Held IDENTICAL -- byte-identical, not merely
# equivalent -- across every target in this family on purpose: the MECHANISM
# THAT PRODUCES THE ONE EXTRA BYTE is the only variable, so a protection
# difference would confound the measurement. The negative control shares this
# exact file too, which is what makes its refusal attributable to the absence
# of the off-by-one rather than to different hardening.
#
# THE ONE FLAG THAT IS LOAD-BEARING FOR THE BUG, STATED PLAINLY RATHER THAN
# BURIED: -fno-stack-protector.
#
# -fno-stack-protector   REQUIRED by three of the five positives, and the
#                        reason is worth recording because it is NOT the usual
#                        "the canary would catch us". A one-byte write onto the
#                        LOW BYTE OF THE SAVED RBP does not touch the canary at
#                        all -- the canary lives at -0x8(%rbp), which is BELOW
#                        the byte being written, so the epilogue's check would
#                        pass. What -fstack-protector actually does to this
#                        category is MOVE THE TARGET: it relocates local arrays
#                        to the bottom of the frame so that they end at the
#                        canary slot instead of at the saved RBP, and the one
#                        extra byte then lands on the canary's low byte and
#                        aborts in __stack_chk_fail. So the canary defeats the
#                        saved-RBP pivot by relayout, not by detection -- and it
#                        does NOT defeat the other two positives at all
#                        (obo_12's length guard and obo_13's saved pointer both
#                        live INSIDE the same struct as the overflowed array, so
#                        no amount of frame reordering separates them). That
#                        asymmetry is a real property of the category and is the
#                        reason this flag is held off for the whole family
#                        rather than varied inside it.
#
# EVERY OTHER PROTECTION IS ON, AND NONE OF THEM MATTERS:
#
# -no-pie                the ret2win destination has to be a FIXED address,
#                        because the primitive here delivers no leak: the frame
#                        is pivoted into a buffer the attacker already filled,
#                        and the word that gets executed had to be written
#                        before the pivot happened. PIE would not make the bug
#                        unreachable, it would make the exploit a two-stage one
#                        (leak, then pivot) and the family would then be
#                        measuring leak plumbing instead of the off-by-one. Held
#                        constant, and named as a simplification rather than
#                        presented as a limitation of the defect.
# -Wl,-z,relro,-z,now    Full RELRO write-protects the GOT after startup. No
#                        step of any of the five routes writes the GOT: the
#                        destinations are a saved RBP, a saved length, a saved
#                        pointer, and a return address -- all of them stack or
#                        .data. Full RELRO is satisfied and irrelevant.
# (NX)                   comes from the default ELF program headers. Nothing is
#                        executed from a data page; every word the pivot lands
#                        on is an address INSIDE .text (a `ret` gadget or
#                        `win`), which is exactly what makes a one-byte write
#                        enough on an NX target.
#
# -O0                    load-bearing for the MEASUREMENT, not for the bug, and
#                        in two distinct ways. First, the family has to be a
#                        controlled comparison, and at higher -O gcc is free to
#                        keep the index in a register, unroll the copy, or turn
#                        the whole inclusive loop into a memcpy -- which would
#                        change the mechanism under test rather than the
#                        protection. Second, the executor DERIVES the geometry
#                        it needs from the frame: which rbp-relative slot the
#                        one extra byte lands on (`-X(%rbp)` plus the fill
#                        bound N), and therefore whether that slot is the saved
#                        RBP, a length, or a pointer. At -O2 the buffer can live
#                        in a register and the derivation would have nothing to
#                        match, so the executor would be guessing.
-fno-stack-protector
-no-pie
-Wl,-z,relro,-z,now
-O0
